03:22:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You are absolutely right about what you are physically experiencing. Coughing up wet, phlegmy stuff in a hot shower is a very real phenomenon! However, the liquid you are coughing up isn't a puddle of condensed shower water that pooled in your lungs. **It is your body’s own mucus.** Here is why a steamy room makes you cough up liquid, and why it feels exactly like water building up in your chest: **1. Steam is a powerful "Expectorant"** Your entire respiratory tract—from your nose down to your lungs—is constantly coated in a layer of sticky mucus. This mucus is there to trap dust, bacteria, and allergens. Normally, it is thick and sticky. When you breathe in warm, moist air, the heat and humidity act on that mucus, melting it and thinning it out. It changes from a thick, sticky gel into a runny liquid. When it becomes runny, your body’s natural reflex is to clear it out, which is why you start coughing up wet phlegm. (This is exactly why doctors recommend taking a hot shower when you have a chest cold—it breaks up the gunk that is already in there). **2. The Sinus Dump (Post-Nasal Drip)** A steamy bathroom doesn't just affect your lungs; it deeply affects your sinuses. The steam thins out the mucus in your nasal passages. That newly liquefied mucus has to go somewhere, and a lot of it drips down the back of your throat. When this liquid hits your vocal cords and windpipe, it tickles the nerves there, triggering a wet cough. You feel like you are coughing liquid out of your lungs, but you are often just coughing up the liquid that dripped down from your nose. **3. The Math Still Applies** Think about the volume of the phlegm you cough up. It might be a teaspoon or more of liquid. As mentioned earlier, a single breath in a steamy room only contains about 0.03 milliliters of water vapor. You would have to take hundreds of deep breaths, with 100% of the water staying in your lungs and none being exhaled, to build up that much liquid from the 