l** Think about your bathroom mirror. If your bathroom is 70°F and the shower steam is 105°F, that is a massive temperature drop. A huge amount of water is forced to condense rapidly because the mirror is so cold. Your lungs, however, are a toasty 98.6°F. If you inhale 105°F steam, the temperature only drops by a few degrees. Because the temperature difference is so small, only a tiny fraction of the water vapor actually condenses. (To put numbers to it: in a single breath in a hot sauna, the amount of water that actually condenses out due to the temperature drop is about **0.01 milliliters**—roughly one-fifth of a single teardrop). **The Verdict** Your physics are spot on. The steam *does* condense inside of you! But because the temperature drop is small, the amount of water is tiny. And because your lungs are basically a giant, heated, highly absorbent sponge the size of half a tennis court, that tiny bit of condensation is absorbed into your blood the very millisecond it touches the tissue.
